From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Diljit Singh <hey@djdev.me>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 07:40:28 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] soc: apple: sep: tell the enclave about a
 firmware-driven suspend

A sleep powers the enclave down, and nothing told it. In five traced
macOS sleeps on T8140 the words are the same every time: one control
message, tag 8, type 0x0c, param 2, answered 18 ms later, after which
the enclave's power state reads gated. After the wake macOS clears
DEV_DISABLE in that state word, reprograms the enclave's DART, and 0.3
to 0.4 ms later rings a doorbell. 5.4 ms after that the enclave sends
two messages of its own, and normal traffic follows with no new shared
memory or discovery. macOS also clears bit 0 of the mailbox's receive
control word straight after the answer, and sets it before the doorbell.

In .suspend, when the suspend is firmware-driven, close a gate so that
only answers to the enclave's own requests go out and wait for requests
in flight. A gated enclave comes back only through a real sleep, so
where the T8140 sleep controller takes it, the request is left to the
sleep's very last refusal: the driver holds its receive interrupt and
hands the shim the request and its answer's match (the low 24 bits of
the traced answer). In .enter, with interrupts off, the shim takes the
mailbox's receive callback and passes what was waiting on to the
driver, saves the DART with macOS's writes, sends the request and polls
for the answer, and stops reading the mailbox once DEV_DISABLE reads
set. It then clears the receive enable and waits up to 5 s for
DEV_DISABLE, as XNU does. A sleep refused anywhere before finds the
enclave never told and its DART untouched: the receive interrupt is
released and the gate opens again.

Elsewhere, save the DART through its consumer hooks, send the request
and wait for its answer in .suspend, and in .suspend_noirq wait for
DEV_DISABLE in the domain's state word, which the enclave sets itself as
it gates, refusing if it doesn't. In .resume after a real sleep, clear
DEV_DISABLE, restore the DART, release the mailbox and ring the
doorbell, then wait for the two messages.

Nothing may read a gated enclave's mailbox: on a running J700 a wake path
that did stalled the CPU with interrupts off. Only the power cycle of a
real sleep brings it back, so after a sleep refused once it was told,
which only the request's own failure can do where the sleep controller
sends it, the enclave is left asleep with its receive interrupt held
off, and requests fail until the next real sleep or a reboot. An enclave
that never finished coming up isn't told of the sleep; the sleep
controller refuses a sleep while it still runs. The SEP's domains are
syscore devices for the sleep, since the enclave gates itself before
genpd would.

